<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leoš Janáček</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leoš Janáček was a Czech composer, born at Hukvaldy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, who spent most of his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, where he founded an organ school. Drawing on Moravian folk music and the rhythms of Czech speech, he wrote operas such as Jenůfa, Káťa Kabanová and The Cunning Little Vixen, as well as the Sinfonietta and the Glagolitic Mass. International fame came only in his last decade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leos-janacek.jpg" alt="Leoš Janáček" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unkonwn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leo%C5%A1_Jan%C3%A1%C4%8Dek_el_1914.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leoš Janáček was born in Hukvaldy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, on 3 July 1854, the son of the schoolmaster Jiří Janacek and Amálie Janáčková. His family had limited means, and his father wanted him to become a teacher but deferred to his evident musical ability. In 1865 he enrolled as a ward of the foundation of St Thomas's Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, where he sang in the choir under Pavel Křížkovský.</p><p>In 1874 he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> organ school, where he lived in poverty, practising on a keyboard drawn on his tabletop. A published criticism of the director's performance of a Gregorian mass led to his expulsion, but he was readmitted and graduated on 24 July 1875 with the best results in his class.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> he taught music and from 1876 taught at the Teachers' Institute, where his pupil Zdenka Schulzová, the director's daughter, later became his wife. In 1881 he founded the organ school and directed it until 1919, when it became the Brno Conservatory.</p><p>From the early 1890s he led the folklorist activity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and Silesia, and he composed piano accompaniments for more than 150 folk songs. His first opera, Šárka, dates from 1887-1888. Around the turn of the century he began to combine his folk studies with his transcriptions of the speech melodies of spoken Czech, which gave his vocal lines their distinctive character.</p><p>His daughter Olga died in February 1903, and he dedicated the opera Jenůfa to her memory. It was performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> in 1904, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> refused it for twelve years; Karel Kovařovic, whose opera he had sharply criticised in 1887, was director of the National Theatre. The revised Jenůfa was accepted there in 1916 and brought Janáček his first acclaim.</p><p>In 1917 he met Kamila Stösslová, who inspired the rest of his work, including Káťa Kabanová, The Cunning Little Vixen, the Sinfonietta and the Glagolitic Mass. Most of his output, and his best-known works, belong to the last decade of his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In August 1928 he caught a chill on an excursion to Štramberk, which developed into pneumonia, and he died on 12 August 1928 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava">Ostrava</a> at the age of 74. A large public funeral included music from the last scene of The Cunning Little Vixen, and he was buried in the Circle of Honour at the Brno Central Cemetery. After his death the Australian conductor Charles Mackerras promoted his work on the world opera stage and restored some compositions to their original forms. Janáček is considered one of the most important Czech composers, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonin-dvorak">Antonín Dvořák</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedrich-smetana">Bedřich Smetana</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leoš Janáček” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cultural life was rich: Karel Čapek, <b>Leoš Janáček</b>, Jaroslav Hašek, the Czechoslovak New Wave in film and later Milan Kundera and Václav Havel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mendel's work in Brno became a foundation of genetics after its rediscovery in 1900; Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk, first president of Czechoslovakia, was born in Hodonín in 1850 and Sigmund Freud in Příbor in 1856; and the composer <b>Leoš Janáček</b> made his career in Brno.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hukvaldy, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in Moravská Ostrava, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.62,18.22&amp;year=1902" aria-label="Hukvaldy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Leoš Janáček"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4352 5825h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4352 5825h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.62,18.22&amp;year=1902">What was here in 1902? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
